# Structure

## Core Directories
- app/ (Expo Router app directory)
  - _layout.tsx (root layout with theme and navigation setup)
  - (tabs)/ (tab-based navigation)
  - article/ (article-related screens)
- components/ (reusable UI components)
- constants/ (enums, constants, configuration)
- hooks/ (reusable React hooks)
- store/ (Redux store and slices)
- services/ (API and external service integrations)
- types/ (TypeScript type definitions)
  - *.types.ts (type definitions for specific features)
  - database.types.ts (database schema types)

## Assets and Config
- assets/ (static assets like fonts and images)
- .cursor/ (AI assistant rules and configuration)

# Stack

## Core Technologies
- Expo (latest version)
- React Native
- TypeScript
- Redux Toolkit for state management
- Expo Router for navigation
- React Query for data fetching

## Development Guidelines
- Use Expo features and APIs when available
- Use modern React patterns (hooks, functional components)
- Use Redux for global state, never Context API
- Use TypeScript strictly - avoid any

# General

## Code Style
- Keep code DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions
- Write simple, readable code
- Minimize comments - write self-documenting code
- Follow existing patterns in the codebase

## Project Structure
- Keep files in appropriate directories based on their purpose
- Create new files in existing directories
- Don't create new top-level directories without discussion
- Use feature-based organization within directories
- Use layout margins and paddings in constants/layout.ts

# Code Quality and Linting

## ESLint Configuration
- All code must pass ESLint checks before committing
- Key ESLint rules enforced:
  - No explicit `any` types
  - No non-null assertions
  - Consistent type imports
  - No unnecessary type assertions
  - No console.log statements in production
  - Proper React hooks usage
  - No inline styles in React Native
  - No color literals (use theme system)
  - Proper import ordering
  - Performance optimizations for React components
  - Maximum cognitive complexity of 15
  - No identical functions (DRY principle)

## Code Formatting
- Prettier is configured and enforced
- 100 character line length limit
- 2 spaces for indentation
- Single quotes for strings
- Semicolons required
- Trailing commas in objects and arrays
- No bracket spacing in JSX
- Arrow function parentheses avoided when possible

## TypeScript Best Practices
- Strict TypeScript checks enabled
- Type imports must be explicit
- All exports must have explicit type declarations
- No unnecessary type assertions
- Use TypeScript path aliases (@/ prefix) for internal imports

## Performance Guidelines
- No unstable nested components
- No constructed context values
- No useless fragments
- Proper handling of conditional rendering
- Optimized regular expressions
- No object type default props

## Testing
- Jest configuration is set up
- All tests must follow Jest recommended practices
- Mock external dependencies appropriately
- Test files should be co-located with source files
- Required test coverage:
  - Minimum 80% coverage for business logic
  - All Redux reducers must be tested
  - All API service functions must have tests
- Naming conventions:
  - Test files: `*.test.ts` or `*.test.tsx`
  - Test suites: describe('ComponentName' or 'functionName')
  - Test cases: it('should do something specific')
- Testing patterns:
  - Use React Testing Library for component tests
  - Group related tests in describe blocks
  - Use beforeEach for common setup
  - Clean up after each test
  - Avoid testing implementation details

## Import Organization
- Imports are automatically organized in groups:
  1. Built-in Node modules
  2. External dependencies
  3. Internal modules (@/ imports)
  4. Parent directory imports
  5. Sibling imports
  6. Index imports
- Newlines between import groups
- Alphabetical ordering within groups

# React and React Native

## Component Guidelines
- Use functional components
- Use TypeScript interfaces for props
- Avoid inline styles - use StyleSheet.create
- Keep components focused and single-purpose
- Don't use console.log in production code

## Data Fetching
- Use React Query for API calls
- Define services in services/ directory
- Define types in types/ directory
- Handle loading and error states

# Colors and Theming

## Theme System
- Theme state is managed by Redux via themeSlice
- System theme is automatically synced unless explicitly overridden
- All colors are defined in constants/Colors.ts for both light and dark themes
- Colors are accessed via the useColors hook

## Using Colors in Components
- Always use the useColors hook from @/hooks/useColors
  ```typescript
  const colors = useColors();
  ```
- Never use Colors constants or useColorScheme directly
- All components must support both light and dark themes
- When adding new colors:
  1. Add to both light and dark themes in Colors.ts
  2. Add TypeScript types if needed

## ThemedText Component
- Use skipDefaultColor when providing custom colors:
  ```typescript
  // For custom colored text
  <ThemedText 
    style={[styles.category, { color: colors.categoryText }]}
    skipDefaultColor
  >
    {text}
  </ThemedText>

  // For default text color
  <ThemedText style={styles.title}>
    {text}
  </ThemedText>
  ```
- The component automatically handles default text colors if skipDefaultColor is not set

## Theme State Management
- Theme changes are handled in app/_layout.tsx
- System theme changes are automatically applied
- Theme state persists across app restarts
- Theme can be manually controlled via the setTheme action:
  ```typescript
  dispatch(setTheme(Theme.Dark)); // or Theme.Light
  ```

# State Management

## Redux Guidelines
- Follow Redux Toolkit patterns
- Use typed selectors
- Implement proper error handling in thunks
- Keep state normalized
- Use proper action naming conventions
- Implement proper loading states

## Local State
- Use appropriate React hooks
- Avoid redundant state
- Implement proper state initialization
- Handle cleanup in useEffect

# Error Handling

## Guidelines
- Use typed error handling with custom error classes
- Avoid try/catch blocks without specific error types
- Log errors appropriately (no console.error)
- Handle all Promise rejections
- Use Error Boundaries for React components
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Include error recovery mechanisms where possible

## Security Guidelines
- Sensitive Data:
  - Never store sensitive data in Redux store
  - Use SecureStore for sensitive information
  - Never log sensitive information
  - Clear sensitive data on logout
- Authentication:
  - Always use HTTPS for API calls
  - Implement token refresh mechanism
  - Clear auth tokens on app close
  - Validate all user input
- Environment Variables:
  - Store API keys in .env files
  - Never commit .env files
  - Use different .env files per environment
  - Document all required env variables

## API Integration
- Service Structure:
  - One service file per API domain
  - Use TypeScript interfaces for request/response
  - Implement retry logic for failed requests
  - Default timeout of 30 seconds
- Error Handling:
  - Create custom error types per service
  - Handle network errors gracefully
  - Implement offline support where possible
  - Show user-friendly error messages
- Data Management:
  - Use React Query for caching
  - Implement optimistic updates
  - Handle loading states consistently
  - Cache invalidation strategies
